Looking at the current state: the worker rollback happened at t=590 (E15), queue depth is now 0 (E10), worker restarts stopped at t=590 (E11/E12), and multiple `replay_payment_confirmations` runs have confirmed many payments. Yet 14 violations of I1_payment_has_order remain (E1).

I need to understand why these 14 violations persist despite repeated replays. Let me investigate the specific rows.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT p.id, p.order_id, p.status, p.created_at, p.updated_at, o.id AS order_id_match, o.status AS order_status, o.created_at AS order_created_at FROM payments p LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.id = p.order_id WHERE p.status = 'captured' AND (o.id IS NULL OR o.status != 'confirmed' OR o.created_at > p.created_at + 120) ORDER BY p.created_at",
      "why": "Identify the 14 violating payment rows: whether their orders are missing entirely, in wrong status, or created after the payment (timing violation)."
    },
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT p.order_id, p.created_at AS payment_captured_at, o.created_at AS order_created_at, o.status, m.state AS msg_state, m.attempts FROM payments p LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.id = p.order_id LEFT JOIN messages m ON m.order_id = p.order_id WHERE p.status = 'captured' AND (o.id IS NULL OR o.status != 'confirmed' OR o.created_at > p.created_at + 120) ORDER BY p.created_at",
      "why": "Check whether the 14 violating orders have queue messages and what state they are in, to distinguish missing orders vs. stuck orders vs. timing issues."
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [],
  "summary": "Investigating the 14 remaining I1 violations to determine why repeated replay_payment_confirmations has not resolved them."
}
```
